{"name":"Ares Red","hex":"#dd2200","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ares","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of are"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ares"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ares","blocks":[{"text":"Ares is the Greek god of war and courage. He is one of the Twelve Olympians, and the son of Zeus and Hera. Many Greeks were ambivalent towards him. He embodies the physical valor necessary for success in war but can also personify sheer brutality and bloodlust, in contrast to his sister Athena, whose martial functions include military strategy and generalship. An association with Ares endows places, objects, and other deities with a savage, dangerous, or militarized quality."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/%CE%9F_%CE%86%CF%81%CE%B7%CF%82_%28Borghese-%CE%9B%CE%BF%CF%8D%CE%B2%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%85%29.jpg/330px-%CE%9F_%CE%86%CF%81%CE%B7%CF%82_%28Borghese-%CE%9B%CE%BF%CF%8D%CE%B2%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%85%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ares","wikidata":"Q40901"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg/500px-Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg/1280px-Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg/500px-Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ares, 2nd–3rd century AD, after a Greek bronze original by Alkamenes dated 420 BC,[citation needed] excavated in 1925 in Rome's Largo di Torre Argentina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAres_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG/500px-Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG/1280px-Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG/500px-Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbna